# Who must review changes to what.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so the broad rule comes first and the specific
# ones override it. Code owners must have write access to this repository --
# an entry naming someone who does not, or a mistyped username, is ignored
# silently rather than reported.
#
# Changing anything under /schemas/, /tools/ or /.githooks/ changes what CI
# enforces. That is a governance event, not a content change, which is why they
# are owned separately from the corpus even though the catch-all already covers
# them.
#
# Two owners on every path, deliberately. With one owner nothing is reviewable:
# GitHub never requests review from a pull request's own author, so a sole owner
# is assigned to nothing and approves nothing.

*                       @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi

# The declaration of authority owns itself. Without this line, whoever
# CODEOWNERS was written to constrain can edit it unreviewed.
/.github/               @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi
/CODEOWNERS             @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi

# The written protocol.
/AGENTS.md              @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi

# What the checks enforce.
/schemas/               @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi
/tools/                 @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi
/.githooks/             @shsagnik @Anuraag-Tripathi
